WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.300
- There is way too much
hype and misinformation

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.480
surrounding this novel coronavirus,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.501
and I wanna talk about it on
this week's Wednesday Check.

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:11.501
(soft upbeat music)

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.230
Let me start off by thanking
each and everyone of you

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.120
who sent me an email or message

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.890
convincing me to make this video.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.350
I didn't need any convincing.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.090
I just didn't wanna
make this video too soon

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.840
because I didn't have enough information

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.190
to give you practical useful guides.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.640
Even now there's still
a lot that we don't know

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.860
but I'm gonna be talking about
that throughout this video.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.060
Let's start off with
that term coronavirus.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.260
There are many different types

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.390
of coronaviruses, different strains.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.650
In fact the common cold that you and I get

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.960
can be caused by a coronavirus.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.230
Now what's going on in Wuhan, China

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.870
is a novel coronavirus.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.320
It's not the one that
causes the common cold.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.760
What makes it novel?

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.130
Well, that's because it's spread

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.160
from an animal carrier to a human.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.140
The current thought is that

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.270
it either came from a bat or a snake.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.930
And this can only happen
if humans are in direct

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.120
close proximity with those animals.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.360
First you have this animal

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.660
that's infected with
this novel coronavirus.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.880
It mutates, then enters a human cell,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.350
starts replicating within the human body,

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.340
thereby infecting that individual

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.980
causing them to have symptoms.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.600
What are the symptoms of
this novel coronavirus?

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.300
The most common, fever,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.850
next is cough, next is
shortness of breath.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.020
Just like the common cold or the flu,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.500
this virus spreads in the same way,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.010
through airborne particles,
surface particles,

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.900
or even close contact with other humans

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.080
who are also infected.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.760
I want you to know that
all the information

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.530
I'm about to present to you here

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.970
comes directly from the CDC or the WHO

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.430
or the Department of Health.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.880
Usually they all work
in coordination together

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.410
but these are your main
sources of information.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.350
Please don't think that if
you read someone's tweet

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.320
and it has been retweeted a lot,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.430
it's an accurate source of information.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.870
It is not.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.130
Look for reputable organizations

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.500
to get your information from.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.530
Also I'd like for you to keep in mind,

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.710
all the statistics and all the information

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.200
I'm giving you make sense right now.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.380
They're up-to-date to this given moment

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.210
but all those things can change.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.010
Tomorrow there's gonna be
more individuals affected

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.270
than they are today.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.980
We may learn something about the virus

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.240
that we didn't know today in a week.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.380
So please keep in mind that this video

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.690
is being filmed on January 28th 2020.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:25.680
I want you to keep this
phrase in your head,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.170
alert but not anxious.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.390
This Wuhan virus or novel coronavirus

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:33.470
is really a potential threat

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.000
to us here in the United States

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.040
and is not a crisis yet by any means.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.700
So again, alert, not anxious.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.070
So let's talk about some of
the stats that we know now.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.780
Currently there's about
4,500 individuals infected

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:49.300
with the novel coronavirus
throughout the world

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.530
with just over 100 deaths.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:52.780
The majority of these,

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.640
the huge majority of these are in China.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.120
In the US we have five total
confirmed cases, zero deaths.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.810
Internationally there are about 30 cases.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.260
Again these numbers are
gonna change quite rapidly.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.660
This is just to give you a feeling

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.980
for what's going on in
this moment right now.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.550
Now you may be wondering to yourself,

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.670
is this a dangerous virus?

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.340
Well, the answer is it depends.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.600
It can become severe in about 20% of cases

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:21.070
leading to pneumonia or acute
respiratory distress syndrome

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.250
leading to ventilation or even
death in some circumstances.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.260
For those of you who are living in the US,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.070
this is certainly a potential threat

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.230
we should be on the lookout for.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.870
With all the international travel

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.440
that goes on in the world today,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.270
it's very easy to spread a virus

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.960
from one country to another.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.040
That being said,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.240
with only five cases here in the US

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.140
with zero deaths thus far,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.810
this is not a crisis and
not a reason to panic.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.540
There's been many headlines in media

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.940
that are trying to
scare you into panicking

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.560
'cause it urges you to click,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.380
and when you click, they earn money.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.670
Do not fall for this.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.620
Look for reputable
sources like CDC or WHO.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.650
I'm gonna keep stating
that over and over again.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.190
There's still a lot of information

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.740
we don't know about
this novel coronavirus,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.660
how long it takes to spread,

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.780
what is the true incubation period,

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.940
meaning that you have the virus

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.000
but you don't show any symptoms yet.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.430
Right now we're thinking
it's a week to two weeks

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.120
but we're still figuring that out.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.940
And then finally can you spread the virus

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.670
during that asymptomatic period

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.440
which would be really dangerous

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.020
because you can have
individuals who are not sick

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.230
actually transmitting the
virus across the world.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.260
In fact when we had a
similar outbreak like this

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.230
in the early 2000s with SARS,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.100
the reason we were able to contain it

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.080
was that we were screening people to see

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.550
who had a fever, who was
sick with this virus,

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.620
and we're able to contain and
isolate those individuals.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.200
But if this virus can be
transmitted even when we're healthy

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.670
or at least seemingly healthy,

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:48.990
that can be quite dangerous.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:50.970
We don't know the answer
to that question yet.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.840
When it comes to treatment
for the coronavirus,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.760
I'm sad to say there is no treatment.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.300
It doesn't mean we can't do anything,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.200
we can still give supportive care.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.010
That means if you're dehydrated,

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.550
we can give you IV fluids.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.210
That means if you have a very high fever,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:05.910
we can give you antipyretics.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.380
If you can't breathe on your own,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.860
we can put you on a ventilator.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.000
So there are certain
things we can help you

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.240
in a supportive manner

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.830
but there's no direct treatment.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.000
Because for example if you
had a bacterial infection,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.910
we can give you an antibiotic,
that's a direct treatment.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.290
There are some companies that
are working on treatments

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.110
for this and are hoping
to make progress very soon

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:26.380
but it's not gonna be done
in the next few weeks.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:30.870
Also the NIH did announce that
they're working very quickly,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.670
very quickly on a vaccine for this.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.140
They're hoping to develop this

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.680
within the next few months

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.340
to have it ready for us

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.050
in case this truly becomes
a worldwide pandemic.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:43.790
Let's talk about what
to do if you're worried

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.120
that you may have this novel coronavirus.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.220
First of all, you would have to have been

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.650
exposed to someone in China,

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.930
like you had to be in the Wuhan area,

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.690
or have been in contact
with someone from that area

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.170
within the last two weeks.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.370
Otherwise it's highly, highly unlikely

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.560
that you have this virus.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.130
But let's say you are still worried,

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.630
what's the first thing you should do?

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.130
Call your healthcare provider

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.430
before going to get care, why?

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:09.590
So they can prepare the waiting area

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:12.520
so that they can isolate
it as best as possible,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:14.610
take you to the proper
protocols to make sure

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.270
that those who are susceptible
are out of harm's way

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.840
because you don't wanna get people sick

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.460
if you truly do have this virus.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.490
Next, they'll ask you
a series of questions

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.050
to see if you meet the
criteria for further testing.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.940
If you do, they'll send out a sample

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.680
to the CDC headquarters
in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.680
That test takes about 24
to 48 hours to complete.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.540
It's called the PCR test.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:35.373
During that time

00:06:35.373 --> 00:06:37.760
you'll stay in an isolation
area in the hospital

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.850
which is usually a negative pressure room

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.070
to prevent the virus
particles from escaping.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:45.110
Again, whether you have
the novel coronavirus,

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.200
the flu, or any other virus,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.430
the treatment remains supportive care.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.890
We're gonna do everything in our power

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.130
to make sure that your
body's immune system

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.870
can fight off whatever virus

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.460
is affecting you in a given moment.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.650
In fact what we're doing
with all our patients

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.020
in the hospitals and primary care offices

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.710
is when someone comes in with symptoms

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.500
that mimic the novel coronavirus,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.880
we do ask a thorough travel history,

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.270
if they've been to China,

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.990
do they have any friends or family

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.630
staying with them that came from China.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.190
These are important questions
that we need to ask.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.010
In fact this screening protocol

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.560
has actually increased in the
United States to 20 airports.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.360
Here are some practical
advise to keep you safe

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.670
from this novel coronavirus
as in any other infections

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.010
that are out there in a given moment.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:28.560
It is flu season after all

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.400
and the flu virus is also very dangerous.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.270
Number one, stay alert but not anxious.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:36.470
I've said it before, I'm saying it again

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.470
'cause I do not want you to panic.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.260
Panic yields false alarms
and that yields bad outcomes.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.000
We don't want that in this case.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.080
Two, do not travel to
China or the Wuhan area

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.830
unless it's absolutely,
absolutely mandatory.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.840
Three, if you were in China
within the last two weeks

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.420
and you're experiencing fever,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.990
cough, or shortness of breath,

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.290
please contact your healthcare
provider as soon as possible.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:01.730
Four, make sure to wash your hands

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:03.260
frequently throughout the day.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:06.430
That means 20 seconds with soap and water.

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:07.590
If that's not available,

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.440
you can use an alcohol-based
hand sanitizer of course.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.790
Five, do your best in avoiding

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.540
touching your mucus membranes.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.080
That includes your eyes,
nose, and your mouth.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:20.010
These bad boys right here
are the most common areas

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:21.820
for infected particles,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:23.950
and then when you put them
on your mucus membranes,

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:25.200
that's how they get in your body.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.120
Six, if you're gonna cough or sneeze,

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.730
do it into a tissue and
then throw that tissue out.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.930
Seven, clean high traffic areas.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:34.840
If you're gonna be using
a public workspace,

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.240
wipe it down first.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.670
It's a simple rule of thumb

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:40.010
that I like to recommend
to all of my patients.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.560
Eight, do your best to avoid contact

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:43.830
with those who are sick.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.760
If you see someone coughing or sneezing,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.620
do your best to keep at least
six feet in between you.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:49.930
That's generally how far

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:51.890
sneeze and cough particles can travel.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.950
Nine, if you're sick, please stay home.

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:58.080
It doesn't matter if you have
the flu or the common cold,

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.720
by staying home, you're decreasing
the spread of that virus,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.590
you're decreasing viral load,

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:07.030
you're making the community
safer by staying home.

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.460
I know we all wanna be more
productive and get to work,

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.880
but you're not gonna do great
work when you're already sick.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.790
A lot of folks have
been asking me questions

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.380
about wearing surgical masks in public,

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.170
whether or not that's beneficial.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:19.580
There can be some benefit there

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:20.970
but there could also be some harm

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.500
because when you're taking the mask off,

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.480
if you get some of the
germs on your hands,

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.050
then you shake hands with somebody,

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.250
now you're spreading that virus further.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.190
So there's kind of pros and
cons in both directions.

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:34.130
The CDC is not making this
general recommendation

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.610
that everyone in a public
place should wear this mask.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.640
And I think we should
stick to that advise.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.730
If we don't need to wear them, don't,

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.057
because the masks that we wear

00:09:42.057 --> 00:09:45.480
in the healthcare
environment are N95 masks.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.170
These are fit-tested masks.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:49.240
They have a very specific
method of working.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:50.840
And the regular surgical masks

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:51.920
that you see people using,

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.380
the disposable ones,

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:55.600
those don't offer a huge
amount of protection.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.420
Just remember because this
is a developing situation,

00:09:58.420 --> 00:09:59.810
details are gonna change,

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.300
that's why I'm gonna link
really good quality sources

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:05.310
from the CDC and the NIH down below.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.070
Click on those for further reading.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:08.560
If you have any questions or comments,

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:09.840
leave them down below.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:12.380
I'm gonna do my best to
stay as active as possible

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.060
in the comments sections
answering your questions

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:17.890
in case this situation
changes or develops further.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.870
Here's a good playlist
if you like to learn

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.980
more about some really cool medical cases.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:25.300
And as always, you're gonna
stay happy and healthy

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.288
when you click on one of these.

